{
 "interest": "This file provides a precise snapshot of the Imperial Japanese Navy's international footprint and intelligence network in early 1933, compiled directly from the official Japanese Naval Register shortly before Japan's withdrawal from the League of Nations. It reveals how Japan allocated naval personnel abroad, heavily weighting technical, ordnance, engineering, and construction specialists in major industrial naval powers (the United States, Great Britain, France, and Germany) to observe naval technological developments. Furthermore, it details the extensive naval officer presence deployed across major Chinese cities and the newly created puppet state of Manchukuo, illuminating early Japanese military-political coordination in East Asia. Several officers listed, such as Captain Takazumi Oka and Commander Shigeru Onishi, subsequently held prominent leadership commands during the Pacific War.",
